1
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.133
In the previous lesson,
you gave feedback to your agent as text.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:09.100
You'll now provide feedback to the agent
in spoken natural language.

3
00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:11.700
You will add multimodal
capabilities to your agent

4
00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:14.800
so it can capture the feedback
as audio spoken by the user.

5
00:00:15.033 --> 00:00:16.600
Let's have some fun!

6
00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:20.733
So far, we've set up a moderately complex
workflow with the human feedback loop.

7
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.100
Let's run it through our visualizer
to see what it looks like.

8
00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:27.100
We're going to bring in a couple of things
just to get that done.

9
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:30.933
Here are all our imports
and our cloud keys. And here,

10
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.200
all in one step is our entire RAG workflow.

11
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.700
This is exactly the workflow
that we were using in the previous lesson,

12
00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:40.933
so there's nothing new to walk through.
We'll execute all three of those.

13
00:00:41.333 --> 00:00:44.600
And then we'll visualize
it using the visualizer.

14
00:00:56.633 --> 00:00:57.800
It's pretty complicated.

15
00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:00.800
So let's zoom out a bit.

16
00:01:02.933 --> 00:01:03.600
Cool.

17
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:07.400
This is a really fun visualization
because you can see the entire happy path

18
00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:08.433
that we're walking through.

19
00:01:08.433 --> 00:01:12.533
You start, you set up, you parse your form,
you generate questions.

20
00:01:12.533 --> 00:01:15.000
You ask a question,
you fill in the application form.

21
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.700
This is the external step where you get
human feedback and then you get feedback.

22
00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:23.533
And at that point you can either stop or
you can complete the loop and do it again.

23
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:27.000
Now, just for fun,
we're going to do one more thing.

24
00:01:27.133 --> 00:01:30.800
You're going to change the feedback
from text feedback to actual words

25
00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:32.300
spoken out loud.

26
00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:36.100
To do this, we'll use a different model
from OpenAI called Whisper.

27
00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:36.933
LlamaIndex

28
00:01:36.933 --> 00:01:40.800
is a built in way to transcribe
audio files into text using Whisper.

29
00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:45.400
Here's a function that takes a file
and uses Whisper to return just the text.

30
00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:46.933
It loads the file.

31
00:01:46.933 --> 00:01:51.933
It instantiates the Whisper reader and
then, it gets the text out of the document.

32
00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:55.400
These are the same sorts of documents
that we were using for RAG earlier.

33
00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:56.633
Before we can use it.

34
00:01:56.633 --> 00:01:59.333
We need to capture some audio
from your microphone.

35
00:01:59.333 --> 00:02:01.033
That involves some extra steps.

36
00:02:01.033 --> 00:02:05.400
First, let's create a callback function
that saves data to a global variable.

37
00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:08.400
So we have somewhere to store
the transcription once we've got it.

38
00:02:08.633 --> 00:02:12.433
Now, you're going to use Gradio,
which has special widgets that can render

39
00:02:12.433 --> 00:02:16.400
inside a notebook to create an interface
for capturing audio from a microphone.

40
00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:19.633
When the audio is captured,
it calls transcribe speech

41
00:02:19.633 --> 00:02:23.200
on the recorded data
and calls store transcription on that.

42
00:02:23.333 --> 00:02:24.900
You can see that happening here.

43
00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:28.100
It's defining your inputs,
defining your outputs.

44
00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:32.000
And it's running this function, which is
the function that we just described up here.

45
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:34.700
And then we're passing
the output of transcribe speech

46
00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:38.200
to store transcription,
which we defined here. In Gradio,

47
00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:40.400
you further define a visual interface

48
00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:42.700
containing this microphone
input and output.

49
00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:44.300
And then you launch it.

50
00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:46.433
So we're creating a blocks interface.

51
00:02:46.433 --> 00:02:48.500
We're putting some tabs into it.

52
00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:50.300
And then we're going to launch it.

53
00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:54.700
Gradio gives us this really cool
already set up interface for us.

54
00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.200
And it can listen to our microphone.

55
00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:58.100
Let's try that out.

56
00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:02.700
Hey computer, can you hear me?

57
00:03:03.333 --> 00:03:04.433
Will submit it.

58
00:03:04.433 --> 00:03:07.433
And it correctly transcribes the audio.

59
00:03:07.433 --> 00:03:09.700
Nice. Gradio is pretty wild.

60
00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:13.900
You can do all of this and print out the
transcription in just a few lines of code.

61
00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:17.733
Let's make sure that we've got it in that
global variable that we set up earlier.

62
00:03:18.833 --> 00:03:19.900
There it is.

63
00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:21.800
We're going to want to run Gradio again.

64
00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:25.033
So it's a good idea to shut down
the Gradio interface that you were using.

65
00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:28.100
Otherwise your Gradio interfaces
will compete with each other.

66
00:03:28.333 --> 00:03:32.500
Now we're going to create an entirely
new class, a transcription handler.

67
00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:35.500
I'm going to walk through what it does
step by step.

68
00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:39.200
First, we're going to create a queue
to hold our transcription values.

69
00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:42.500
Every time we record something
we're going to put it in the queue

70
00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:44.900
using this method, store transcription.

71
00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:47.500
This create interface
method is the same interface

72
00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:50.500
and transcription logic
that we were using a little bit earlier,

73
00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:53.500
except it stores the result in a queue
instead of a global.

74
00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:54.733
You can see that happening

75
00:03:54.733 --> 00:03:58.100
because it's calling self-store
transcription, which we defined here.

76
00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:02.100
Then, we launch the transcription interface
just like we did before.

77
00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:06.300
But the new thing we do
is we pull every 0.5 seconds

78
00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:08.433
waiting for
something to end up in the queue.

79
00:04:08.433 --> 00:04:11.433
So this, while true
loop, will just continue forever

80
00:04:11.633 --> 00:04:14.600
sleeping for 5.5 seconds every time.

81
00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:17.400
But if there's something in the queue,
it'll notice

82
00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:20.100
and it will close the interface
and return the result.

83
00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:21.733
Now, you have a transcription handler.

84
00:04:21.733 --> 00:04:25.700
You can use it instead of the keyboard
interface when you're getting human input

85
00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:27.133
when you run your workflow.

86
00:04:27.133 --> 00:04:30.133
We don't have to change the workflow
at all for this to work.

87
00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:32.700
So just like before
we set up our workflow.

88
00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:35.700
We pass in our fake resume
and our fake application form,

89
00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:38.600
and we wait for an input-required event.

90
00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:42.433
Now, instead of getting keyboard input,
we call our transcription handler.

91
00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:44.400
Once our transcription handler

92
00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:48.000
has given us a transcription,
we send that as the human response event.

93
00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:51.333
So the moment of truth. Let's try it out.

94
00:04:51.333 --> 00:04:53.633
A change that I made
that I didn't tell you about

95
00:04:53.633 --> 00:04:57.100
was to give us a little bit more feedback
about what's going on under the hood.

96
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:00.500
When I told it to do is to print out

97
00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:03.600
every question and every answer that
it gets while it's thinking.

98
00:05:04.733 --> 00:05:07.033
So you can see it doing that here.

99
00:05:07.033 --> 00:05:07.433
All right.

100
00:05:07.433 --> 00:05:10.433
It's got to the end of the questions.

101
00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:14.800
And now it's spun up

102
00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:17.800
a Gradio interface
asking us for feedback.

103
00:05:17.933 --> 00:05:20.300
We can see from the answers
to the questions

104
00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:23.000
that it's made the same mistake
that it made before.

105
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:27.200
The project portfolio is a list of things
that the candidate did instead of a URL.

106
00:05:27.933 --> 00:05:31.033
So let's use the audio feedback to tell it

107
00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:34.200
that that's what we need to change.

108
00:05:35.033 --> 00:05:38.033
The portfolio field should be a URL.

109
00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:46.200
We submit

110
00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:48.200
and the LLM reads that transcript

111
00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:51.300
and correctly decides
that I gave it some feedback.

112
00:05:51.833 --> 00:05:54.833
So now you can see our questions
and answers happening.

113
00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:58.000
Each one of them has this extra feedback
field attached.

114
00:05:58.133 --> 00:05:59.600
Every single question

115
00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:02.900
gets the same feedback
that the portfolio field should be a URL,

116
00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:05.000
which is really only useful
for one of them.

117
00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:06.700
You can think about how you would improve

118
00:06:06.700 --> 00:06:10.333
that if you were doing this
in a more production application.

119
00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:13.200
Okay,
it got to the end of the questions again,

120
00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:16.300
and it spun up another Gradio
interface asking us for feedback.

121
00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:20.200
Let's tell it that
it did a good job this time because it did

122
00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:23.200
the portfolio as a URL,
just like we asked.

123
00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:27.300
That's great.

124
00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:30.300
Good job.

125
00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:37.233
The LLM has correctly interpreted
my positive feedback

126
00:06:37.233 --> 00:06:40.233
as saying that everything is okay,
and it spat out the form.

127
00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:41.933
Congratulations!

128
00:06:41.933 --> 00:06:46.233
You've successfully created an AI agent
that responds to spoken human feedback.

